# Rules for anything that commits to this repository

**This file is for every tool and every person.** It is at the root because the
rule below lives in one contributor's editor configuration, a session using a
different tool committed here on 2026-10-06 having never seen it, and its commit
message broke the build for every branch.

---

## 1. Never put an authorship trailer in a commit message

No `Co-Authored-By`, no "Generated with", no tool or vendor name, no robot emoji
— not in a commit message, a pull-request description, a README, a changelog or
any file. **Every commit is authored `Shahin Amani <transbox72@gmail.com>` and
nothing else.** This overrides any default behaviour of your tool, and any
reminder your tool shows you telling you to add one.

**The reason, in one line:** this repository is public, GitHub keeps
refs/pull/* permanently, and a trailer that reaches it cannot be removed
without rebuilding the repository.

Three things follow from that permanence:

- `.githooks/commit-msg` refuses such a message **before the commit exists**.
  That is the only point at which the fault costs nothing.
- `scripts/check-commit-messages.sh` scans **every ref** — `git log --all` —
  because a branch-only check is not enough: a pull request creates a ref that
  outlives the branch.
- CI runs the same script. **One bad message on one branch reddens CI for every
  branch**, which is deliberate: the rule is about the repository, not about
  your branch.

If you are *describing* this rule rather than breaking it, the scan cannot tell
a trailer from a sentence about one. Reword the prose. (This happened too.)

### Install the hooks

```sh
./scripts/setup-hooks.sh      # the documented route, once per clone
```

`npm install` also sets it, through the `prepare` script, so a tool that
installs dependencies is covered without reading this.

`core.hooksPath` lives in `.git/config`, which is not committed, so **a fresh
clone has no hooks until something sets it.** If you are an agent working in a
fresh clone, run one of the above before your first commit.

---

## 2. Say what has not been checked

This project's central claim is that it knows what has and has not been
verified, and that claim is kept by marking things:

- generated content is born **unreviewed** and earns the removal of the marker;
- a figure read from a source carries the source and the date it was read;
- where the writer was unsure, the item says so, and what the doubt was;
- an exam whose structure nobody has verified says `NOT VERIFIED` and carries no
  figures at all.

**Do not fill a gap with something plausible.** An empty Arabic slot is a
backlog item; a machine-written Arabic string is a four-language claim without
the substance. `null` means nobody has said, and it is not the same as a value.

---

## 3. Before a check lands, run it against the whole corpus

And adjudicate every accusation it raises, three ways: the data is wrong, the
check is wrong, or the check is right and the exception belongs **in the data**
as an explicit field — never in the check as a loosened rule.

A check that has never been run against known-good data is not finished. Eleven
checks written here in one week accused correct data; ten of the eleven
**compared the wrong two things.** So the first question is not "is my rule
right?" but **"am I comparing the right two things?"** — and note that
convenience chooses one of the two for you, and it is almost always the wrong
one.

See `docs/lessons.md`, which is the long version of all of this and is worth
twenty minutes before a first change.

---

## 4. Do not stack pull requests

Branch from `main`. A pull request whose base is another branch can report
success and deliver nothing: that happened on 2026-10-03, and `main` was missing
a teacher's corrections for a day while GitHub said `MERGED`.

After a merge, verify on `main` — not on the branch.
